  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{\u2033}+b(x){y}^{\u2032}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x){y}^{\u2033}-x{y}^{\u2032}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+2{y}^{\u2032}+y=2\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=2,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+xy-2x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{\u2033}+b{y}^{\u2032}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+xy=2x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=2x,$ which is different from zero.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
